data: {"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Returning 1 articles (offset 0)\n\n**Article 1: ‘Russophobia’: a narrative for every occasion**\n\n**ID:** 231812\n\n**Content:**\nThe Kremlin uses ‘Russophobia’ as an all-purpose narrative to dismiss evidence of Russian interference, discredit Western governments, justify claims of escalation and corruption, and portray the countries most exposed to Russian pressure as irrationally hostile to Moscow.\n On 15 November, Estonian Foreign Minister Margus Tsahkna announced that Estonia would close all checkpoints on its border with Russia from 1 December. The move followed what Estonian authorities described as clear evidence that Russia’s military intelligence service, the GRU, had ordered a sabotage attack against an Estonian defence company. A day earlier, Estonia had detained eight Latvian citizens suspected of acting on Russian instructions and planning an arson or explosive attack against the Threod Systems drone factory in Viimsi, near Tallinn.\n Caught out? Cry ‘Russophobia’\n Russia responded with a familiar routine. First came denial: Moscow claimed that the detained Latvians had nothing to do with the alleged sabotage and that Estonia had provided no proof. Then came the inevitable blame-shifting. Foreign Ministry spokesperson Maria Zakharova said that, if Tallinn really intended to close the border, the decision would fit into ‘yet another bout of aggressive and unrestrained Russophobia’.\n Claiming ‘Russophobia’ - an irrational fear or dislike of Russia or Russians – may be one of the Kremlin’s most useful inventions. It’s a wonderfully versatile narrative: whenever Moscow has no convincing answer to accusations or evidence of Russian interference, it can declare them ‘Russophobic’ without addressing the matter further.\n One narrative, many uses\n The wider Russophobia narrative comes with its own set of useful subplots. One is distinctly anti-elite: ordinary Europeans are supposedly not hostile to Russia, but are being manipulated by politicians and the media into being ‘Russophobic’. Implying deliberate manipulation by governments is useful enough on its own for encouraging distrust in public institutions, and from there, the narrative is easily expanded further: if politicians are manufacturing Russophobia, they must be doing it for a reason.\n One convenient explanation is money. Russophobia can supposedly be used to justify higher military spending – naturally, followed by corruption. A recent RIA Novosti article followed exactly this logic, claiming that British authorities had made Russophobia profitable by inventing Russian spy threats and then introducing a new tax to pay for the response.\n Russophobia can also be presented as a pretext for escalation. Russian Deputy Foreign Minister Sergei Ryabkov recently made that connection, claiming that the West had turned Russophobia into ‘a hot commodity’ and ‘a driver of militarisation’, while openly preparing for a possible conflict with Russia. At this point, one major Kremlin narrative neatly merges with another: Russophobia becomes proof of the ‘warmongering West’.\n Another supposed benefit is political distraction. According to pro-Kremlin outlets, Western leaders exaggerate the Russian threat because it helps them hide their failures at home. This fits neatly into the familiar ‘decaying West’ narrative: governments unable to solve domestic problems supposedly need an external enemy to keep their citizens looking elsewhere.\n And once Russophobia has been established as politically and financially useful, the next logical step is to claim that neighbouring countries have institutionalised it. Poland’s Centre for Eastern Studies was recently cast as little more than a state-funded factory of Russophobia after publishing a report on Russia’s weaknesses. Pro-Kremlin outlets claimed that the report was designed not to analyse Russia, but to harm ordinary Russians and destabilise the Russian state. In this narrative, even research into Russia becomes evidence of hostility towards it.\n The closer to Russia, the more ‘Russophobic’\n Looking at the history of “Russophobia” claims, one pattern stands out: the countries accused most often tend to be those closest to Russia. Poland, the Baltic states and Finland are among the usual suspects. They also happen to be frequent targets of Russian sabotage, interference and other forms of hybrid aggression. In an even starker example, Moscow accused Ukraine of ‘Russophobia’ for years before launching its full-scale invasion – and now continues to use the label to justify its aggression.\n As the saying goes, just because you’re paranoid doesn’t mean no one is following you. In this case, fearing Russia does not automatically make the fear irrational. Nor should accusations of ‘Russophobia’ stop anyone from looking more closely at what caused them in the first place – namely, Russia’s own actions.\n The ‘Russophobia’ narrative as a FIMI tool\n These examples are more than isolated expressions of anti-Western sentiment. They illustrate how the Kremlin uses information manipulation to shape perceptions, undermine trust and influence political debate in countries it considers adversaries. The recurring pattern is to distort or dismiss verifiable events, attribute hostile motives to legitimate government decisions, portray defensive measures as aggression, and sow suspicion about the intentions and integrity of public institutions.\n The claims are amplified through state-controlled and pro-Kremlin media, adapted to different audiences and linked to other established narratives, including the ‘corrupt West’, the ‘warmongering West’ and the ‘decaying West’. Their cumulative effect is to obscure Russia’s own actions, weaken public support for measures taken in response to Russian threats, and create divisions within Western societies and between European countries. This is precisely what makes the ‘Russophobia’ narrative a FIMI tool: it is not simply criticism of Western policy, but a recurring, coordinated pattern of manipulative messaging that serves Russia’s strategic interests.\n Don't be deceived.\n\n**Date:** 2026-11-20 10:08:54\n\n**Categories:** Disinformation Review\n\n**Keywords:** Russophobia\n\n**Languages:** English\n\n**Source URL:** https://euvsdisinfo.eu/russophobia-a-narrative-for-every-occasion/\n\n\n**Articles Info:**\n- Total articles: 11964\n- Current page: 1 of 11964\n- Has next page: Yes\n"}],"structuredContent":{"total":11964,"offset":0,"limit":1,"articles":[{"id":"231812","title":"‘Russophobia’: a narrative for every occasion","content":"<p><strong>The Kremlin uses ‘Russophobia’ as an all-purpose narrative to dismiss evidence of Russian interference, discredit Western governments, justify claims of escalation and corruption, and portray the countries most exposed to Russian pressure as irrationally hostile to Moscow.</strong></p> <p>On 15 November, Estonian Foreign Minister Margus Tsahkna announced that Estonia would <a href=\"https://vm.ee/en/news/foreign-minister-tsahkna-estonia-close-road-border-crossings-russia\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">close all checkpoints</a> on its border with Russia from 1 December. The move followed what Estonian authorities described as clear evidence that Russia’s military intelligence service, the GRU, had ordered a sabotage attack against an Estonian defence company. A day earlier, Estonia had detained eight Latvian citizens suspected of acting on Russian instructions and planning an arson or explosive attack against the Threod Systems drone factory in Viimsi, near Tallinn.</p> <h3><strong>Caught out? Cry ‘Russophobia’</strong></h3> <p>Russia responded with a familiar routine. First came denial: Moscow claimed that the detained Latvians had nothing to do with the alleged sabotage and that Estonia had provided no proof. Then came the inevitable blame-shifting. Foreign Ministry spokesperson Maria Zakharova said that, if Tallinn really intended to close the border, the decision would fit into ‘yet another bout of aggressive and unrestrained Russophobia’.</p> <p>Claiming ‘<a href=\"https://euvsdisinfo.eu/the-terrible-russophobia/\">Russophobia</a>’ - an irrational fear or dislike of Russia or Russians – may be one of the Kremlin’s most useful inventions. It’s a <a href=\"https://euvsdisinfo.eu/the-russophobia-myth-appealing-to-the-lowest-feelings/\">wonderfully versatile narrative</a>: whenever Moscow has no convincing answer to accusations or evidence of Russian interference, it can <a href=\"https://euvsdisinfo.eu/report/western-journalism-is-dominated-by-russophobia-and-prefers-fantasy-and-story-telling-to-objective-reporting/\">declare</a> them ‘Russophobic’ without addressing the matter further.</p> <h3><strong>One narrative, many uses</strong></h3> <p>The wider Russophobia narrative comes with its own set of useful subplots. One is distinctly anti-elite: ordinary Europeans are supposedly not hostile to Russia, but are being <a href=\"https://archives.stratctools.eu/archived/13b27a0f5e024cc683195443b71323de/001_tass.com_2211995.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">manipulated</a> by politicians and the media into being ‘Russophobic’. Implying deliberate manipulation by governments is useful enough on its own for encouraging distrust in public institutions, and from there, the narrative is easily expanded further: if politicians are manufacturing Russophobia, they must be doing it for a reason.</p> <p>One convenient explanation is money. Russophobia can supposedly be used to justify higher military spending – naturally, followed by corruption. A recent RIA Novosti article <a href=\"https://archives.stratctools.eu/archived/13b27a0f5e024cc683195443b71323de/002_uk.news-pravda.com_181501.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">followed exactly this logic</a>, claiming that British authorities had made Russophobia profitable by inventing Russian spy threats and then introducing a new tax to pay for the response.</p> <p>Russophobia can also be presented as a pretext for escalation. Russian Deputy Foreign Minister Sergei Ryabkov recently <a href=\"https://archives.stratctools.eu/replay/?source=https%3A%2F%2Farchives.stratctools.eu%2Freplay%2Fcollections%2Fd99ae3882040416ca6636baaf69776a2%2Fruns%2Ff5908744c8924f6fb795ab8e25108f12%2Farchive.wacz#view=replay&amp;url=https%3A%2F%2Fen.topwar.ru%2F293229-rjabkov-arhitektura-bezopasnosti-v-evrope-razrushena-i-ne-podlezhit-vosstanovleniju.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">made that connection</a>, claiming that the West had turned Russophobia into ‘a hot commodity’ and ‘a driver of militarisation’, while openly preparing for a possible conflict with Russia. At this point, one major Kremlin narrative neatly merges with another: Russophobia becomes proof of the ‘<a href=\"https://euvsdisinfo.eu/report/there-is-no-russian-hybrid-war-it-is-the-eu-and-nato-that-want-to-wage-war-against-russia/\">warmongering West</a>’.</p> <p>Another supposed benefit is political distraction. According to pro-Kremlin outlets, Western leaders exaggerate the Russian threat because it helps them <a href=\"https://archives.stratctools.eu/archived/87eb69f19d4d4589a61450747434adab/002_usa.news-pravda.com_945406.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hide their failures at home</a>. This fits neatly into the familiar ‘decaying West’ narrative: governments unable to solve domestic problems supposedly need an external enemy to keep their citizens looking elsewhere.</p> <p>And once Russophobia has been established as politically and financially useful, the next logical step is to claim that neighbouring countries have institutionalised it. Poland’s Centre for Eastern Studies was recently cast as little more than a <a href=\"https://archives.stratctools.eu/archived/87eb69f19d4d4589a61450747434adab/001_francais.news-pravda.com_1060699.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">state-funded factory of Russophobia</a> after publishing a report on Russia’s weaknesses. Pro-Kremlin outlets claimed that the report was designed not to analyse Russia, but to harm ordinary Russians and destabilise the Russian state. In this narrative, even research into Russia becomes evidence of hostility towards it.</p> <h3><strong>The closer to Russia, the more ‘Russophobic’</strong></h3> <p>Looking at the history of “Russophobia” claims, one pattern stands out: the countries accused most often tend to be those closest to Russia. <a href=\"https://euvsdisinfo.eu/report/polands-new-russophobic-military-doctrine-calls-for-unprovoked-attacks-on-russia/\">Poland</a>, the <a href=\"https://euvsdisinfo.eu/report/russophobia-on-the-rise-in-the-baltic-states-at-the-behest-of-the-anglo-saxons/\">Baltic states</a> and <a href=\"https://yle.fi/a/3-9271906\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Finland</a> are among the usual suspects. They also happen to be frequent targets of Russian sabotage, interference and other forms of hybrid aggression. In an even starker example, Moscow <a href=\"https://euvsdisinfo.eu/report/poland-the-baltic-states-and-ukraine-are-infected-by-russophobia/\">accused</a> Ukraine of ‘<a href=\"https://euvsdisinfo.eu/report/russophobia-has-become-an-official-ideology-in-ukraine/\">Russophobia</a>’ for <a href=\"https://euvsdisinfo.eu/report/russophobia-has-become-a-normal-thing-in-ukraine/\">years</a> before launching its full-scale invasion – and now continues to use the label to justify its aggression.</p> <p>As the saying goes, just because you’re paranoid doesn’t mean no one is following you. In this case, fearing Russia does not automatically make the fear irrational. Nor should accusations of ‘Russophobia’ stop anyone from looking more closely at what caused them in the first place – namely, Russia’s own actions.</p> <h3><strong>The ‘Russophobia’ narrative as a FIMI tool</strong></h3> <p>These examples are more than isolated expressions of anti-Western sentiment. They illustrate how the Kremlin uses information manipulation to shape perceptions, undermine trust and influence political debate in countries it considers adversaries. The recurring pattern is to distort or dismiss verifiable events, attribute hostile motives to legitimate government decisions, portray defensive measures as aggression, and sow suspicion about the intentions and integrity of public institutions.</p> <p>The claims are amplified through state-controlled and pro-Kremlin media, adapted to different audiences and linked to other established narratives, including the ‘corrupt West’, the ‘warmongering West’ and the ‘decaying West’. Their cumulative effect is to obscure Russia’s own actions, weaken public support for measures taken in response to Russian threats, and create divisions within Western societies and between European countries. This is precisely what makes the ‘Russophobia’ narrative a FIMI tool: it is not simply criticism of Western policy, but a recurring, coordinated pattern of manipulative messaging that serves Russia’s strategic interests.</p> <p>Don't be deceived.</p> ","dateGmt":"2026-11-20 10:08:54","keywords":[{"term_id":323,"slug":"russophobia","name":"Russophobia","parent":0,"term_taxonomy_id":323,"term_order":0,"facet":"{\"term_id\":323,\"slug\":\"russophobia\",\"name\":\"Russophobia\",\"parent\":0,\"term_taxonomy_id\":323,\"term_order\":0}"}],"categories":[{"term_id":17,"slug":"disinformation-review","name":"Disinformation Review","parent":0,"term_taxonomy_id":17,"term_order":0,"facet":"{\"term_id\":17,\"slug\":\"disinformation-review\",\"name\":\"Disinformation Review\",\"parent\":0,\"term_taxonomy_id\":17,\"term_order\":0}"}],"articleLanguage":[{"term_id":1828,"slug":"en","name":"English","parent":0,"term_taxonomy_id":1828,"term_order":0,"facet":"{\"term_id\":1828,\"slug\":\"en\",\"name\":\"English\",\"parent\":0,\"term_taxonomy_id\":1828,\"term_order\":0}"}],"thumbnailURL":null,"url":"https://euvsdisinfo.eu/russophobia-a-narrative-for-every-occasion/"}],"currentPage":1,"totalPages":11964,"hasNextPage":true}},"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":3}

